        'INDUSTRY STYLE (native technical editor): Claim no more than the English proves: drop eşsiz, benzersiz, rakipsiz, mükemmel, sektörde eşsizdir unless a fact backs them. Units as on Turkish spec sheets: bar first (psi in brackets), d/d, l/dk, kg/h, m³/h (never /s); 1.500 kg, 0,5 mm, %100, 6-10 kat. CTAs use the formal imperative of the English verb, verb last: \'X hakkında fiyat teklifi için mühendislik ekibimize ulaşın\'. One term per concept on a page: devir for rotor speed, debi for measured flow, kartuş for the turbo core. Break English chains: no \'X, Y ve Z ile donatılmış olup\' runs, no 3-deep possessive stacks, no filler \'olarak\'. Headings, tabs, spec labels and bullets in sentence case; model names (UNI-64, Winbal) and ISO/EN codes unchanged.',
        'A MACHINE OR SYSTEM NAME IS REBUILT, NOT TRANSLATED WORD BY WORD: front every English qualifier that trails the head noun or hangs off "with", and never let it land on the neighbouring noun. "Vertical balancing machine standard" is "Standart dikey balans makinesi"; "Engine cooling fan with motor balancing machine" is "Motorlu motor soğutma fanı balans makinesi"; "Fan and motor balancing machine" is "Fan ve motor balans makinesi", never "Fan ve motorlu balans makinesi". Inside a NAME the head is "balans makinesi", never the process noun "balanslama makinesi". A card lead "<Brand> <Product> system for <use> applications." becomes "<Brand> <Product> sistemi, <use> için tasarlanmıştır." If the Turkish shows three -ı/-i/-u/-ü possessive heads in a row, rebuild it; render "a wide range of X" as "çok çeşitli X", never "geniş bir X yelpazesi"; and never gloss a word you already translated ("kartuş (core)" is a defect).',
        'AN ENGLISH NOUN USED AS A MODIFIER TAKES -lI/-lU, NOT THE BARE STEM: "roll brake test system" is "rulolu fren test sistemi"; "dual drill correction" is "çift matkaplı düzeltme"; "half case RDU/PTU" is "yarım gövdeli RDU/PTU"; "residential appliances" is "konut tipi cihazlar". Express features and parts the same way, with -lI or "...olan", never "...-e sahip": "kendi muylusu olan rotorlar", "dört kanallı sistem", "su soğutmalı", "servo tahrikli". Never use "sahip olmak" for credentials or abstractions: degrees are "almak" or "mezun olmak", opportunities "elde etmek", capabilities "kazanmak". A span takes the ablative-dative pair: "tek kanallıdan 60 kanallıya kadar". Never reproduce an English hyphen chain word for word: "<X>-as-a-service" inverts to "hizmet olarak balanslama (BaaS)"; "end-of-line" is "hat sonu" with no genitive. Coordinate two nouns with "ve", not "ile" ("gürültü ve titreşim", "servis ve bakım"); keep "ile" for a genuine instrument or the outer members of a nested list.',
        'A SPEC OR FEATURE BULLET IS A NOUN PHRASE, AND AN ADJECTIVE STAYS AN ADJECTIVE RATHER THAN GAINING A VERB: "Check relief pressure" in a check list is "Tahliye basıncı kontrolü", never "Tahliye basıncını kontrol edin"; "Easily add new models" is "Yeni modellerin kolayca eklenmesi"; "Compatible with most common leak detectors" ends in "...dedektörleriyle uyumlu", never the noun "uyumluluk". A description of what we deliver never becomes an imperative: "Periodic verification of machine operations" is "Makine çalışmasının periyodik doğrulaması". "Interfaces with X" is "X ile entegre çalışır", never "arayüzlendirme"; "Accommodates multiple valve models" is "...modeline uyum sağlar", not "destekler". Rebuild a clause where English elides the verb and keep one shape across a sibling list. A duty, role or service list describing what the company does stays third person: "Identify, establish, and develop new fleet users" is "...belirler, kazanır ve geliştirir", not "belirleyin".',
